/**
\page page_grid_statistics Grid statistics and other global mesh information


## Grid statistics

Besides the actual vertex/face/cell data your solver attaches to the mesh,
Peano's core continuously tracks a small set of statistics about the mesh
itself while it traverses it: how many cells are refined vs unrefined, whether
the mesh still changes, how fine the finest cells currently are, and so forth.
These data live in @ref peano4::grid::GridStatistics.


### Dataflow

- Every peano4::grid::Spacetree accumulates its own
  @ref peano4::grid::GridStatistics object while it descends through its cells
  during a traversal.
- Each rank typically owns several spacetrees at once. All of them are
  collected within @ref peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet, the singleton that
  owns and traverses all of a rank's local trees.
- At the very end of @ref peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet::traverse(), the set
  sums up the statistics of its local trees via GridStatistics' `operator+`
  (src/peano4/grid/grid.cpp), which adds up the counters, combines the flags
  with a logical or and takes the minimum of the mesh sizes.
- It then all-reduces this sum over all ranks through
  tarch::mpi::Rank::allReduce(), using the very same `operator+` as reduction
  operator, and stores the outcome. Without MPI, this step degenerates to a
  plain copy.

\dot
digraph GridStatisticsDataflow {
  rankdir=LR
  fontname="Helvetica"
  node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fillcolor=white, fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10]
  edge [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=9]

  subgraph cluster_rank0 {
    label     = "rank 0"
    fontname  = "Helvetica"
    fontsize  = 10
    style     = dashed

    Spacetree01 [label="Spacetree #1"]
    Spacetree02 [label="Spacetree #2"]
    SpacetreeSet0 [label="SpacetreeSet", fillcolor=lightgrey]

    Spacetree01 -> SpacetreeSet0 [label="1: accumulate\nwhile descending"]
    Spacetree02 -> SpacetreeSet0
  }

  subgraph cluster_rank1 {
    label     = "rank 1"
    fontname  = "Helvetica"
    fontsize  = 10
    style     = dashed

    Spacetree11 [label="Spacetree #3"]
    SpacetreeSet1 [label="SpacetreeSet", fillcolor=lightgrey]

    Spacetree11 -> SpacetreeSet1
  }

  GlobalStats [label="GridStatistics\n(same on all ranks)"]

  SpacetreeSet0 -> GlobalStats [label="2: sum up local trees,\n3: all-reduce at end\nof traverse()"]
  SpacetreeSet1 -> GlobalStats
}
\enddot


### All ranks see the same statistics

@ref peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet::getGridStatistics() returns the reduced
statistics, i.e. the same values on every rank. They describe the whole mesh
after the most recent traversal. As every rank runs traverse() at the same
time, the reduction is safe, and decisions taken on the statistics, such as
peano4::grid::isGridStationary() in the repeat condition of a mesh refinement
step, are the same on all ranks. Before the first traversal, getGridStatistics()
returns the statistics of the local trees as set up by
@ref peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet::init().

If you need the statistics of one individual local tree, use
@ref peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet::getGridStatistics(int) const. These are not
reduced.


### A typical use

The statistics can be read out through the spacetree set:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
peano4::grid::GridStatistics stats =
  peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet::getInstance().getGridStatistics();
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

A common place, where this is used, is the default step reporter
@ref peano4::DefaultStepReport::reportStep() which you can use as a
starting point for your own use of the stats:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
const auto stats = peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet::getInstance().getGridStatistics();

logInfo(
  "finishTimeStep()",
  "min mesh size="    << tarch::la::max(stats.getMinH()) <<
  ", #local cells=("  << stats.getNumberOfLocalUnrefinedCells()  << "/" << stats.getNumberOfLocalRefinedCells()  << ")" <<
  ", #remote cells=(" << stats.getNumberOfRemoteUnrefinedCells() << "/" << stats.getNumberOfRemoteRefinedCells() << ")"
);
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

exahype2.api.fv.actionsets.ComputeGlobalCFLCondition
(src/exahype2/api/fv/actionsets/ComputeGlobalCFLCondition.py) is a worked
example: it reads the finest mesh size from the statistics and combines it
with the solver's reduced maximum eigenvalue (@ref exahype2.api.fv.actionsets.ReduceMaxEigenvalue)
into a globally admissible CFL time step size.


### Reducing your own data

The statistics of the whole mesh are already reduced. If you assemble a
GridStatistics object yourself, e.g. from the statistics of some individual
trees, @ref peano4::grid::reduceGridStatistics() combines such an object over
all ranks. It is a blocking routine which every rank has to call at the same
time, so only call it from somewhere that runs once per rank at a well-defined
point, such as a static prepareTraversal()/unprepareTraversal()
(@see @ref page_peano_action_sets).

Not everything you might want a global view of lives inside GridStatistics.
exahype2.api.fv.actionsets.ComputeGlobalCFLCondition's maximum eigenvalue is a
plain `double` the solver accumulates itself (guarded by its own static
tarch::multicore::BooleanSemaphore while several trees/threads on the same
rank may still be writing it). For a plain scalar like that, use
tarch::mpi::Rank::getInstance().allReduce() directly, guarded by
`#ifdef Parallel` (the call needs MPI-specific types that do not exist in a
non-MPI build). Do **not** call raw `MPI_Allreduce` directly:
tarch::mpi::Rank::allReduce()'s own documentation explains why a literal
blocking allreduce risks deadlocking Peano's asynchronous message exchange,
and what the `waitor` callback
(`tarch::services::ServiceRepository::getInstance().receiveDanglingMessages()`
in the example below) is for. As tarch::mpi::Rank::allReduce()'s documentation
requires, only one thread per rank may call it.

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
double local  = /* your own locally-accumulated value */;
double global = local;
#ifdef Parallel
tarch::mpi::Rank::getInstance().allReduce(
  &local, &global, 1, MPI_DOUBLE, MPI_MAX,
  [&]() -> void { tarch::services::ServiceRepository::getInstance().receiveDanglingMessages(); }
);
#endif
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~


## Load balancing information

@ref toolbox::loadbalancing::Statistics mirrors the grid statistics: its
updateGlobalView(), which every load balancing strategy invokes in its
finishStep(), all-reduces the load balancing's own data over all ranks via
tarch::mpi::Rank::allReduce() - the global number of trees, how many ranks
currently have load balancing switched on, the global number of splits, and the
maximum number of trees held by any one rank. The reduction is blocking, so all
ranks hold the same values afterwards. Only the data explicitly labelled as
local, such as the number of splits a rank has triggered itself, remain
rank-local.

@ref toolbox::loadbalancing::CostMetrics follows the same pattern: its
updateGlobalView() all-reduces the global weight, the lightest rank and the
minimum of the rank weights with blocking reductions.

The load balancing statistics do not provide cell counts. Strategies which need
the global number of cells read it from
@ref peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet::getGridStatistics(). The load balancing
however also needs the weight of each individual rank, which the reduced grid
statistics do not provide. The strategies and
@ref toolbox::loadbalancing::metrics::CellCount::updateGlobalView() therefore
sum up the number of local unrefined cells over the rank's local trees via
@ref peano4::parallel::SpacetreeSet::getGridStatistics(int) const.


*/
